  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Where does the line $3y-x=7$ meet the circle $(x-3)^2+y^2=10$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(2,3)$.",
      "worked": "From the line, $x=3y-7$. Substitute into the circle:\n$$\\begin{align*}(3y-7-3)^2+y^2&=10\\\\(3y-10)^2+y^2&=10\\\\9y^2-60y+100+y^2&=10\\\\10y^2-60y+90&=0\\\\y^2-6y+9&=0\\\\(y-3)^2&=0\\\\y&=3.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThen\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=3(3)-7\\\\x&=2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe line meets the circle at the single point $(2,3)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
